/* ============================================================================
 * WORD VOYAGERS — YEAR TWO READING (36 weekly passages)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * One passage a week, alternating literature and informational, ~200–270 words,
 * pitched at the grades 4–5 complexity band (roughly 830–1010L). All original
 * to this curriculum; biblical and historical material is retold in our own
 * words, never quoted from a translation.
 *
 * QUESTIONS ARE DELIBERATELY HARDER THAN YEAR ONE'S. At least one question
 * per passage asks the child to identify which QUOTATION supports a claim,
 * because that is the central fifth-grade reading standard and it is the one
 * thing a multiple-choice item can actually test honestly: put the real line
 * beside three plausible near-misses and see whether they can tell.
 *
 * Ids permanent: `la-y2-rd-w<week>-q<i>`.
 * ==========================================================================*/
(function(){

  const P = {

  /* ---- Unit 1 · Say It Exactly --------------------------------------- */
  1:["The Copyist's Error","Literature",
   "Brother Anselm had copied manuscripts for thirty-one years, and he had never once, so far as he knew, changed a word on purpose.\n\nThe novice assigned to him that winter was quick, clever, and impatient. When the boy reached a line that seemed clumsy, he smoothed it. When a sentence repeated itself, he cut the repetition. He was, he explained, improving the text.\n\nAnselm set down his pen.\n\n\"Whose book is this?\" he asked.\n\n\"The abbey's.\"\n\n\"No. Before that.\"\n\nThe boy thought. \"The author's.\"\n\n\"And when a reader in a hundred years finds your smoothed line, whose words will he think he is reading?\"\n\nThe boy was quiet.\n\n\"He will think they are the author's,\" Anselm said. \"He will build an argument on them. He may build his life on them. And they will be yours, put there by a boy who thought he could write better.\"\n\nHe pushed the page across the table.\n\n\"Copy it again. Every clumsy word. If you believe a line is wrong, you may write a note in the margin and sign your name to it. That is honest. What you did is not.\"\n\nThe boy recopied the page. In the margin, in small careful letters, he wrote his first note — and signed it.",
   [["Which quotation shows Anselm's central objection to what the novice did?",['"Whose book is this?"','"They will be yours, put there by a boy who thought he could write better."','"He had copied manuscripts for thirty-one years."','"The boy was quiet."'],1],
    ["What does Anselm permit the novice to do instead of changing the text?",["cut repeated sentences","write a signed note in the margin","skip difficult lines","choose a different manuscript"],1],
    ["What does the word 'novice' most likely mean in this passage?",["an expert copyist","a beginner in training","an abbot","a reader"],1],
    ["What theme does this passage most clearly develop?",["old methods are better than new ones","faithfully representing another's words is a duty, not a preference","monasteries were strict places","young people should not write"],1]]],

  2:["What the Ice Remembers","Informational",
   "Two miles beneath the surface of Antarctica, scientists have drilled out a cylinder of ice that is older than the human species.\n\nIce cores work as records because snow does not simply pile up — it traps things. Each year's snowfall seals in dust, ash, pollen, and, most valuably, tiny bubbles of the actual atmosphere from the year it fell. Compressed under later snow, those bubbles survive unchanged for hundreds of thousands of years.\n\nWhen researchers extract a core, they can count its annual layers much as one counts tree rings. Then they can open the bubbles and measure precisely how much carbon dioxide the air held at any given depth.\n\nThis is why ice cores matter so much to climate science: they do not require anyone to model or estimate past atmospheres. The atmosphere itself is there, sealed in the sample.\n\nThe longest cores recovered so far stretch back roughly 800,000 years, covering eight complete ice-age cycles. Within them, researchers found something striking — a volcanic ash layer that matches, year for year, an eruption recorded in written histories on the other side of the world.\n\nThat match is a kind of proof. Two entirely independent records, one written by humans and one written by weather, agreeing on a date.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the claim that ice cores require less estimation than other methods?",['"Two miles beneath the surface of Antarctica..."','"The atmosphere itself is there, sealed in the sample."','"The longest cores recovered so far stretch back roughly 800,000 years."','"Each year\'s snowfall seals in dust, ash, pollen..."'],1],
    ["How do researchers determine the age of a layer within a core?",["by chemical dating only","by counting annual layers, much like tree rings","by measuring the core's length","by comparing it to other continents"],1],
    ["Why does the author call the volcanic ash match 'a kind of proof'?",["ash is easy to identify","two independent records agree on the same date","volcanoes are well documented","it was the deepest layer"],1],
    ["What are TWO main ideas of this passage?",["Antarctica is cold, and drilling is difficult","Ice cores preserve actual past atmospheres, and independent records can confirm each other","Volcanoes erupt often, and ash travels far","Snow is heavy, and ice is old"],1]]],

  3:["The Argument About the Bridge","Literature",
   "The town meeting had run two hours when Delia's father finally stood.\n\nThe question was whether to repair the old iron bridge or replace it. The replacement faction had spoken well. Their leader, a man named Voss, had said the bridge was \"a century past its purpose\" and that sentiment was no substitute for steel.\n\nDelia's father was not a good speaker. He knew it. He had written his three points on the back of a feed receipt.\n\n\"Mr. Voss says the bridge is a century past its purpose,\" he began. \"I counted. It is eighty-four years old. That is not the same thing, and if his numbers are loose on the easy part, I want to see the hard part twice.\"\n\nSomeone laughed. Voss did not.\n\n\"Second. He says repair costs will exceed replacement within ten years. The engineer's letter says within twenty-two years, and only if the county stops maintenance. That letter is in the file. I read it this morning.\"\n\nHe looked at his receipt.\n\n\"Third. I do not know which is better. I truly do not. But I know we have been given one set of numbers tonight and the file has another, and I would like us to decide with the right ones in front of us.\"\n\nThe vote was postponed one week.",
   [["Which quotation shows the father's actual position on the bridge?",['"It is eighty-four years old."','"I do not know which is better. I truly do not."','"Sentiment is no substitute for steel."','"The vote was postponed one week."'],1],
    ["What is the father's specific objection to Voss's cost claim?",["repairs are always cheaper","the engineer's letter gives a different figure and condition","Voss dislikes the bridge","the county has no money"],1],
    ["What does the father mean by 'if his numbers are loose on the easy part, I want to see the hard part twice'?",["he cannot do arithmetic","an error on a checkable fact makes him doubt the harder claims","the bridge is hard to inspect","Voss is lying about everything"],1],
    ["How does the author use the father's poor speaking ability?",["to show he should not have spoken","to contrast plain accuracy with polished persuasion","to make the reader laugh","to show the town was unfair"],1]]],

  4:["Why Summaries Are Harder Than They Look","Informational",
   "Ask most students to summarize an article and they will hand back the first three sentences, shortened.\n\nA real summary is a different operation. It requires deciding what the text is fundamentally about, which means holding the whole thing in mind at once and then judging what can be cut without damage. That judgment is the skill; the shortening is just the visible result.\n\nResearchers who study reading comprehension have found that summarizing ability predicts later academic performance better than reading speed does. The reason appears to be that summarizing cannot be faked. A student who did not really follow an argument can still recognize its vocabulary, and can still answer a detail question by scanning back. But they cannot compress it, because they never assembled it.\n\nThere is also a difference between summarizing and paraphrasing that trips people up. Paraphrasing restates a passage in different words at roughly the same length. Summarizing restates it at substantially shorter length, keeping only what is essential. A paraphrase of a chapter is another chapter. A summary of a chapter is a paragraph.\n\nBoth require the same underlying honesty: whatever comes out must still be the author's meaning, not yours.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the claim that summarizing reveals genuine understanding?",['"Ask most students to summarize an article..."','"Summarizing cannot be faked."','"A paraphrase of a chapter is another chapter."','"Researchers who study reading comprehension..."'],1],
    ["According to the passage, what is the key difference between paraphrasing and summarizing?",["paraphrasing is dishonest","paraphrase keeps roughly the same length; summary is substantially shorter","summarizing uses quotations","there is no real difference"],1],
    ["What does the word 'compress' most nearly mean here?",["squeeze physically","reduce to essentials while keeping meaning","translate","memorize"],1],
    ["Why can a student answer detail questions without understanding the argument?",["detail questions are unfair","they can scan back and recognize vocabulary","teachers give hints","details are unimportant"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 2 · The Shape of a Story --------------------------------- */
  5:["The Lamp in the Window","Literature",
   "For forty-one years, Mrs. Okonkwo put a lamp in her front window at dusk.\n\nHer son had left for the capital at nineteen, during the troubles, and had written twice in the first year and not again. She did not know whether he was alive. The lamp was so that if he came back at night, down the long road from the junction, he would see which house was still his.\n\nThe village found it sad at first. Then, gradually, they found it ordinary. Children grew up thinking of the Okonkwo house as the one with the light. Travelers on the junction road used it to navigate. A midwife delivering a baby in the dark set her bag down by it. Twice, men who had drunk too much and lost the path found the lamp and then found their own doors.\n\nMrs. Okonkwo died in November, and the village argued about the lamp for a week.\n\nThen a young woman named Chidi, who had been one of the children who grew up navigating by it, began putting a lamp in her own window. She lived on the same road, a quarter mile nearer the junction.\n\nWhen people asked why, she said only that she had gotten used to the light being there.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the idea that the lamp came to serve people beyond its original purpose?",['"Her son had left for the capital at nineteen."','"Travelers on the junction road used it to navigate."','"Mrs. Okonkwo died in November."','"She did not know whether he was alive."'],1],
    ["What theme does this passage most fully develop?",["grief should be kept private","an act of faithful love can outlive its original purpose and shelter others","villages are close-knit","waiting is pointless"],1],
    ["How does the village's attitude toward the lamp change over time?",["from admiration to irritation","from sadness to treating it as ordinary and useful","from fear to acceptance","it never changes"],1],
    ["What does Chidi's action at the end suggest?",["she is waiting for someone too","the practice has been passed on and given new meaning","she wants attention","she disliked Mrs. Okonkwo"],1]]],

  6:["Two Brothers at the River","Literature",
   "The flood took the north field in under an hour.\n\nTobias and Rafe had farmed it jointly since their father died — jointly and badly, since neither would concede a decision to the other. When the water rose, they were two hundred yards apart, and each made a choice.\n\nTobias ran for the equipment. He got the tractor and both pumps to high ground, and stood there watching the water come, calculating what could still be saved.\n\nRafe ran for the Halvorsen place downstream, where an old woman lived alone with no telephone. He carried her out on his back through water to his chest and did not come back for four hours.\n\nWhen the water dropped, Tobias had saved eleven thousand dollars of machinery. Rafe had lost his half of everything portable.\n\nThey did not speak for a month.\n\nWhat finally broke it was not an apology. It was Tobias walking down to Rafe's kitchen in February and saying that he had done the arithmetic and the machinery would have been replaceable, and that he had known that at the time, and had gone for it anyway because it was the decision he could measure.\n\nRafe said he had not thought at all. He said he was not sure that was better.\n\nThey started over from there.",
   [["Which quotation reveals Tobias's honest assessment of his own choice?",['"He got the tractor and both pumps to high ground."','"He had known that at the time, and had gone for it anyway because it was the decision he could measure."','"They did not speak for a month."','"The flood took the north field in under an hour."'],1],
    ["How do the two brothers' responses to the same crisis differ?",["one panicked and one stayed calm","one acted to preserve property, the other to save a person","one worked and one fled","they responded identically"],1],
    ["What does Rafe mean when he says 'he was not sure that was better'?",["he regrets saving the woman","instinct without thought is not obviously superior to calculation","Tobias was right","he wants the machinery"],1],
    ["What does the ending suggest about the brothers' relationship?",["it is permanently broken","honest acknowledgment, not apology, made reconciliation possible","Tobias won the argument","they will farm separately"],1]]],

  7:["How the Printing Press Changed Reading","Informational",
   "Before movable type reached Europe in the 1450s, a book was a major financial asset. A single Bible could take a scribe more than a year, and cost roughly what a house cost.\n\nThe usual account of what changed emphasizes speed and price, and both mattered enormously. Within fifty years, European presses had produced an estimated twenty million volumes. A book that once cost a year's wages could be had for a few days'.\n\nBut a second change is less often noticed and arguably mattered more: printing made copies identical.\n\nWhen every copy was hand-made, every copy differed. Scribes introduced errors, and later scribes copied those errors and added their own. Two readers in different cities could not assume they were reading the same words. Scholarly argument across distance was nearly impossible, because nobody could cite a page number that would mean anything to anyone else.\n\nPrinting fixed the text. Suddenly a scholar in Basel could write that a claim appeared on page 214, and a scholar in Bologna could turn to page 214 and find it. Footnotes, indexes, and cross-references — the entire machinery of shared scholarship — depend on that reliability.\n\nCheapness spread books. Identicalness made them arguable.",
   [["Which quotation states the author's less-obvious main point?",['"Within fifty years, European presses had produced an estimated twenty million volumes."','"Printing made copies identical."','"A single Bible could take a scribe more than a year."','"Cheapness spread books."'],1],
    ["What are the TWO main ideas of this passage?",["Books were expensive, and scribes were slow","Printing lowered cost, and standardized texts made shared scholarship possible","Europe changed in the 1450s, and Bibles were valuable","Scribes made errors, and books were rare"],1],
    ["Why was scholarly argument across distance nearly impossible before printing?",["people could not travel","no two copies were reliably identical, so citations meant nothing","nobody could read","there were no universities"],1],
    ["What does the final sentence, 'Identicalness made them arguable,' mean?",["identical books caused arguments","standardized texts let scholars debate the same evidence","books became controversial","arguing about books became popular"],1]]],

  8:["The Chapter That Ends Too Soon","Informational",
   "Novelists have known for centuries that where you end a chapter changes how a book is read.\n\nThe technique has a name — the chapter hook — and it works on a documented feature of human memory. Psychologists call it the Zeigarnik effect, after a researcher who noticed in the 1920s that waiters remembered unpaid orders in detail and forgot them almost immediately after payment. Unfinished business occupies the mind; completed business is released.\n\nA chapter that ends mid-crisis exploits this directly. The reader closes the book but does not close the question, and the unresolved tension does the author's work overnight.\n\nBut structure does more than generate suspense. The division of a text into chapters also controls pace and emphasis. A very short chapter after several long ones lands like a hammer stroke — the white space around it signals importance without a single word of explanation. Some authors use a one-sentence chapter for exactly this reason.\n\nPoets do the same work with stanzas, and playwrights with scenes. In each case, the reader is being given instructions about how to weight what they just read, encoded not in the words but in where the words stop.\n\nStructure, in other words, is an argument about attention.",
   [["Which quotation explains WHY a chapter hook works?",['"The technique has a name — the chapter hook."','"Unfinished business occupies the mind; completed business is released."','"Some authors use a one-sentence chapter."','"Poets do the same work with stanzas."'],1],
    ["According to the passage, what does a very short chapter after long ones accomplish?",["saves the reader time","signals importance through the surrounding white space","confuses the reader","ends the book"],1],
    ["What does 'exploits' most nearly mean in this passage?",["treats unfairly","makes deliberate use of","destroys","explains"],1],
    ["What does the closing line, 'Structure is an argument about attention,' mean?",["structure is controversial","how a text is divided tells readers what to weight as important","arguments need structure","attention spans are short"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 3 · Two Accounts ----------------------------------------- */
  9:["The Same Winter, Two Letters","Literature",
   "The historical society holds two letters describing the winter of 1888 in the same county, written six days apart.\n\nThe first is from Anna Lindqvist, nineteen, working as a hired girl on a farm eleven miles from town. She writes that the storm came so fast the schoolteacher roped the children together to walk them home, and that she herself lost the barn in the whiteness twice while carrying two buckets forty feet. Her letter is four pages. Two of them are about the cold.\n\nThe second is from Dr. Emil Brandt, the county physician, writing to a colleague in Chicago. He gives the temperature, the wind speed, the estimated dead, and a paragraph on the pattern of frostbite injuries he treated. His letter is one page and mentions his own experience of the storm in a single clause: \"having been myself delayed some hours.\"\n\nA reader wanting to know how cold it was should use Brandt. A reader wanting to know what it was like should use Lindqvist.\n\nNeither is more truthful. They were answering different questions, for different readers, and the difference in what they recorded is not a disagreement — it is the reason historians want both.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the claim that the two letters differ in purpose rather than accuracy?",['"His letter is one page."','"They were answering different questions, for different readers."','"Her letter is four pages."','"The storm came so fast the schoolteacher roped the children together."'],1],
    ["What is the main difference between what the two writers recorded?",["one lied and one told the truth","Lindqvist recorded lived experience; Brandt recorded measurements and medical data","Brandt was there and Lindqvist was not","one wrote later than the other"],1],
    ["Why does the author say historians want both accounts?",["to check for lies","because each answers a question the other does not","because letters are rare","to compare handwriting"],1],
    ["What does Brandt's single clause about his own experience suggest about his purpose?",["he was not affected by the storm","his personal experience was not what he was writing to convey","he was a poor writer","he arrived after the storm"],1]]],

  10:["Two Ways to Explain a Volcano","Informational",
   "Open two books about the 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens and you may find the same facts organized in completely different shapes.\n\nThe first book proceeds chronologically. March: a bulge appears on the north flank. April: it grows five feet per day. May 18, 8:32 a.m.: an earthquake triggers the largest landslide in recorded history, which uncorks the mountain sideways. The reader experiences the event as it unfolded, and the structure builds tension toward a moment.\n\nThe second book is organized by cause and effect. It opens with plate tectonics, explains how subduction generates magma, describes the specific pressures that produce a lateral blast rather than a vertical one, and only then applies all of it to 1980. The reader arrives at the eruption already equipped to understand why it happened as it did.\n\nNeither structure is superior. They serve different purposes and different readers. The chronological book is better for conveying what the event was like; the causal book is better for conveying why volcanoes behave as they do.\n\nA skilled reader notices the structure early and adjusts. If you are reading chronologically and want mechanism, you will be frustrated — not because the book is bad, but because you are asking it a question it was not built to answer.",
   [["Which quotation states the passage's central point about structure?",['"Open two books about the 1980 eruption..."','"They serve different purposes and different readers."','"March: a bulge appears on the north flank."','"A skilled reader notices the structure early."'],1],
    ["How is the second book organized?",["chronologically","by cause and effect","alphabetically","by geography"],1],
    ["According to the passage, why might a reader become frustrated with a well-written book?",["it is too long","they are asking it a question its structure was not built to answer","the facts are wrong","it lacks pictures"],1],
    ["What advantage does the chronological structure offer?",["explaining mechanisms","conveying what the event was like as it unfolded","listing causes","comparing volcanoes"],1]]],

  11:["The Village and the Surveyor","Literature",
   "When the railway surveyor came through in 1901, he recorded the village's population as 340 and its principal industry as subsistence farming.\n\nThe village's own record, kept in a ledger by the schoolmaster, listed 412 people and described the principal industry as weaving.\n\nBoth were accurate.\n\nThe surveyor counted households registered for tax and did not count the seventy-odd seasonal workers who lived in the valley part of the year, because by his definition they belonged elsewhere. He classified industry by what the village sold to outside buyers, and the village sold grain.\n\nThe schoolmaster counted everyone who slept in the valley in a given month, because that was who he might have to teach. He classified industry by what people spent their hours doing, and most people wove — for their own households, for barter within the valley, and for a market that never appeared in any tax record.\n\nA historian using only the surveyor's figure would describe a small farming settlement. A historian using only the ledger would describe a larger weaving community. Both would be citing a real document and both would be wrong about the village.\n\nThe village was two things at once, and each observer had a definition that could only see one of them.",
   [["Which quotation best explains why both records were accurate?",['"Both were accurate."','"Each observer had a definition that could only see one of them."','"The village\'s own record, kept in a ledger by the schoolmaster..."','"Most people wove."'],1],
    ["Why did the surveyor's population count differ from the schoolmaster's?",["he counted badly","he counted only tax-registered households, excluding seasonal workers","the schoolmaster exaggerated","the population changed"],1],
    ["What was the relationship between how each man defined 'industry'?",["they used the same definition","the surveyor counted external sales; the schoolmaster counted how people spent their time","neither defined it","both counted weaving"],1],
    ["What is the author's main point about using historical sources?",["old records are unreliable","a source's definitions shape what it can show, so one source alone can mislead","surveyors were careless","villages were hard to count"],1]]],

  12:["The Story Nobody Told Straight","Literature",
   "Three people saw the accident at the mill gate, and the foreman took all three statements before noon.\n\nWilkes, who had been walking toward the gate, said the cart came through too fast and the driver was not looking. He described the driver's face in detail — a detail that troubled the foreman, since Wilkes had been approaching from behind the cart.\n\nOyelaran, who had been on the loading platform above, said the cart came through at ordinary speed but that the gate itself had been left half-swung, narrowing the passage. She had not seen the driver's face at all and said so.\n\nThe driver, Petit, said he had been going slowly and had called out twice. He also said, in the last line of his statement, that he had been thinking about his daughter's fever and could not now recall whether he had actually called out or only intended to.\n\nThe foreman read all three that evening. He found Oyelaran's most useful — not because she was necessarily the most honest, but because she was the only one who had clearly marked the boundary of what she could see.\n\nThat, he wrote in his report, is what he would want from a witness: not certainty, but an accurate map of their own uncertainty.",
   [["Which quotation states the foreman's standard for a good witness?",['"He found Oyelaran\'s most useful."','"Not certainty, but an accurate map of their own uncertainty."','"The cart came through too fast."','"Three people saw the accident at the mill gate."'],1],
    ["Why does Wilkes's account trouble the foreman?",["he was lying about the speed","he described the driver's face though he approached from behind the cart","he arrived late","he refused to answer"],1],
    ["What makes Petit's statement unusual?",["he denies everything","he admits he cannot distinguish what he did from what he intended","he blames Wilkes","he saw nothing"],1],
    ["How does each witness's position influence their account?",["it does not","what each could physically see and what each was thinking about shaped what they reported","only Oyelaran's position mattered","all three saw the same thing"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 4 · Time and Sequence ------------------------------------ */
  13:["What the Letter Had Already Said","Literature",
   "By the time Junia opened the letter, she had already decided to go.\n\nShe had packed on Tuesday. She had given notice at the shop on Wednesday, and had spent Thursday walking the city saying a private goodbye to buildings that did not know her. The letter arrived Friday.\n\nIt was from her aunt in the north, and it said what Junia had assumed it would say: that there was room, that the work was hard, that she should come.\n\nWhat she had not expected was the last paragraph. Her aunt wrote that she had written a nearly identical letter eleven years earlier, to Junia's mother, and had never sent it. She had judged that her sister would not want to be asked. She had been wrong, she now believed, and had thought about that unsent letter for eleven years.\n\nJunia sat with the page for a long while.\n\nShe had made her decision without the letter, and the decision had not changed. But something else had. She had thought she was leaving. She understood now that she was also being invited — and that the invitation had been waiting, in a drawer, since before she could remember.",
   [["Which quotation shows that Junia's decision preceded the letter?",['"By the time Junia opened the letter, she had already decided to go."','"It was from her aunt in the north."','"Junia sat with the page for a long while."','"She had thought she was leaving."'],0],
    ["What had the aunt done eleven years earlier?",["visited Junia's mother","written a nearly identical letter and never sent it","moved north","sent money"],1],
    ["What changes for Junia by the end of the passage?",["her decision to go","her understanding of what her going means","her destination","her opinion of her aunt"],1],
    ["Why does the author place so many events in the past perfect ('had packed,' 'had given notice')?",["to make it confusing","to establish that these happened before the letter arrived","to slow the story","to show they were unimportant"],1]]],

  14:["The Clock That Made Longitude Possible","Informational",
   "For most of the age of sail, a ship's captain could determine latitude easily and longitude barely at all — and the difference killed thousands of people.\n\nLatitude comes from the sun's angle, which anyone with a sextant can measure. Longitude requires knowing what time it is somewhere else. If a navigator knows the exact time at a reference port and compares it to local noon aboard ship, the difference converts directly to distance east or west.\n\nThe problem was that no clock could keep accurate time at sea. Pendulum clocks, which had solved timekeeping on land, were useless on a rolling deck. By the time a ship had crossed the Atlantic, an error of a few minutes had become an error of dozens of miles.\n\nIn 1714 the British Parliament offered a prize for a solution. Most competitors pursued astronomical methods. A carpenter's son named John Harrison spent forty-three years building clocks instead.\n\nHis fourth attempt, finished in 1759, was a watch about five inches across. On a voyage to Jamaica it lost five seconds in eighty-one days.\n\nHarrison had not made navigation more clever. He had made it unnecessary to be clever — which is usually how a hard problem finally gets solved.",
   [["Which quotation best explains why longitude was so difficult?",['"Latitude comes from the sun\'s angle."','"Longitude requires knowing what time it is somewhere else."','"In 1714 the British Parliament offered a prize."','"His fourth attempt, finished in 1759, was a watch about five inches across."'],1],
    ["Why were pendulum clocks unusable at sea?",["they were too expensive","a rolling deck disrupted the pendulum","they were too large","sailors could not read them"],1],
    ["How long did Harrison work on the problem?",["eighty-one days","five years","forty-three years","a century"],2],
    ["What does the final sentence mean?",["Harrison was not clever","robust solutions that remove the need for skill often beat clever ones","navigation is easy","cleverness is bad"],1]]],

  15:["The Promise Kept Late","Literature",
   "Elias had promised his grandfather that he would finish the boat.\n\nHe made the promise at sixteen, standing in a workshop that smelled of cedar, and he meant it entirely. Then he went to university, and then he took a job in another country, and the boat sat under a tarp for nineteen years.\n\nHis grandfather died in the eleventh year. Elias flew home for the funeral, stood in the workshop for five minutes, and flew back.\n\nWhat finally brought him home for good was ordinary — a job ending, a lease expiring. He was forty-one. The tarp had gone brittle and torn. Beneath it, the hull was sound.\n\nHe worked on it for two years. He was not a skilled woodworker and had to learn, badly and slowly, things his grandfather could have shown him in an afternoon. Several times he did a thing wrong, undid it, and did it again.\n\nBy the following spring he will have spent more hours on the boat than his grandfather did.\n\nThat fact does not redeem the nineteen years. Elias knows it does not. But he has come to think that a promise kept late is a different thing from a promise broken — not as good, not nothing, and worth the distinction.",
   [["Which quotation states the passage's central claim about the promise?",['"Elias had promised his grandfather that he would finish the boat."','"A promise kept late is a different thing from a promise broken."','"The tarp had gone brittle and torn."','"He was not a skilled woodworker."'],1],
    ["Which sentence in the passage uses the future perfect tense?",["He worked on it for two years.","By the following spring he will have spent more hours on the boat than his grandfather did.","His grandfather died in the eleventh year.","He was forty-one."],1],
    ["What does the detail that the hull was still sound suggest?",["the tarp worked well","what his grandfather built lasted, even while neglected","boats are durable","Elias was lucky"],1],
    ["What does Elias conclude about the nineteen years?",["they are redeemed by his work","the delay is not erased, but late faithfulness still differs from failure","they did not matter","he should not have come home"],1]]],

  16:["The Error That Took Eight Years to Find","Informational",
   "In 1999, NASA lost a spacecraft because two engineering teams had used different units.\n\nThe Mars Climate Orbiter had traveled 416 million miles over nine months. As it approached Mars, ground controllers fired its engines to place it in orbit. It came in roughly sixty miles too low, entered the atmosphere, and was destroyed.\n\nThe investigation found that software built by one contractor had produced thruster values in pound-seconds, while the navigation software expected newton-seconds. The two numbers differ by a factor of about 4.45. Nobody had checked.\n\nWhat makes the case genuinely instructive is not the error itself but how long it survived. The mismatch had been present since before launch. Navigators had noticed small unexplained discrepancies during cruise and had filed them, and the discrepancies had been reviewed, and no one had traced them to their cause. The team had grown accustomed to a small anomaly that was, in fact, the signature of a fatal one.\n\nThe report's central recommendation was not about units at all. It was that persistent unexplained discrepancies must be resolved rather than tracked — because a small error you have learned to live with has stopped being information and become background.",
   [["Which quotation states the report's central lesson?",['"Nobody had checked."','"Persistent unexplained discrepancies must be resolved rather than tracked."','"The two numbers differ by a factor of about 4.45."','"The Mars Climate Orbiter had traveled 416 million miles."'],1],
    ["Why does the author say the case is 'genuinely instructive'?",["the spacecraft was expensive","the error survived a long time despite being noticed","Mars is difficult to reach","the units were unusual"],1],
    ["What had navigators observed during the cruise?",["nothing unusual","small unexplained discrepancies that they filed and reviewed","engine failure","communication loss"],1],
    ["What does the passage mean by an error becoming 'background'?",["it disappears","people grow so used to it that it no longer prompts investigation","it moves behind other data","it becomes larger"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 5 · Joining and Separating -------------------------------- */
  17:["The Word Between","Informational",
   "English has about 150 prepositions, and they do a startling amount of work for words that are mostly under six letters.\n\nA preposition establishes relationship. Put a book and a table in a sentence with nothing between them and you have no picture at all. Put on between them and the picture snaps into place — and under, beside, beneath, and against each produce a different one, from identical nouns.\n\nThis is why prepositions are among the hardest features of English for learners. They are rarely logical. You wait for a bus but wait on a customer. You are in a car but on a train. You arrive at a building but in a city. Native speakers cannot usually explain these; they simply know them.\n\nSome prepositions carry more meaning than whole clauses. Consider the difference between a decision made with the committee and a decision made for the committee and a decision made by the committee. Three sentences, identical except for one small word, describing three completely different distributions of power.\n\nA careful writer chooses prepositions as deliberately as verbs. A careless one hands the reader a picture they did not intend.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the claim that prepositions carry substantial meaning?",['"English has about 150 prepositions."','"Three sentences, identical except for one small word, describing three completely different distributions of power."','"They are rarely logical."','"Native speakers cannot usually explain these."'],1],
    ["Why are prepositions difficult for language learners?",["they are very long","their usage is often idiomatic rather than logical","there are only a few","they change spelling"],1],
    ["What does the author mean by saying the picture 'snaps into place'?",["the book falls","the relationship between the nouns suddenly becomes clear","the sentence ends","the image is photographed"],1],
    ["What is the author's main advice to writers?",["avoid prepositions","choose prepositions as deliberately as verbs","use short words","imitate native speakers"],1]]],

  18:["The Interjection at the Trial","Literature",
   "The courtroom had been quiet for three hours when Mrs. Ferrers said \"Oh.\"\n\nIt was not loud. It was one syllable, spoken by a woman in the third row who had not been called as a witness and had no formal part in the proceedings. But it arrived exactly as the prosecutor finished reading the date of the letter, and something in it made the judge look up.\n\nAn interjection is grammatically detached — it belongs to no clause, modifies nothing, governs nothing. Textbooks treat it as a scrap. And yet a single syllable, correctly timed, had just done something no argument in the room had managed: it had communicated recognition.\n\nThe judge asked her to approach.\n\nMrs. Ferrers explained that she had kept a boarding house on Cheapside in the year in question, and that the date read aloud was the date of the fire, and that the man now accused of writing a letter from London that evening had spent it carrying her furniture into the street.\n\nThe letter, examined again, proved to be misdated by a clerk.\n\nThe smallest unit of grammar had overturned the largest claim in the case — not by arguing, but by being an honest involuntary noise made at the right moment.",
   [["Which quotation explains why the interjection mattered?",['"It was not loud."','"It had communicated recognition."','"The courtroom had been quiet for three hours."','"An interjection is grammatically detached."'],1],
    ["How does the author describe an interjection grammatically?",["it governs the sentence","it is detached, modifying and governing nothing","it acts as a verb","it joins two clauses"],1],
    ["What had Mrs. Ferrers recognized?",["the accused man's face","that the date read aloud was the date of the fire","the prosecutor's voice","the judge"],1],
    ["What is ironic about the outcome, according to the final paragraph?",["the trial ended early","the smallest grammatical unit overturned the largest claim","the letter was real","the judge was wrong"],1]]],

  19:["Neither Rain nor Snow","Informational",
   "The phrase carved on New York's main post office — about neither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night staying couriers from their rounds — is not, and never was, an official motto of the United States Postal Service.\n\nIt comes from Herodotus, writing around 430 BC about the mounted messengers of the Persian Empire. A architect chose it in 1912 because it fit the building's frieze.\n\nWhat makes the line memorable is partly its grammar. It is built on correlative construction — neither... nor... nor... nor — which forces every item into the same grammatical shape and gives the sentence a hammering, cumulative rhythm. Each obstacle lands with the same weight as the last. The structure itself conveys relentlessness before the meaning arrives.\n\nWriters have used the device for exactly this purpose for millennia. Correlatives create parallelism, and parallelism creates emphasis. Both... and joins two things and insists neither is subordinate. Not only... but also builds an escalation. Either... or presents a genuine fork.\n\nUsed carelessly, these constructions break: not only reading but also to swim is wrong not because the meaning is unclear but because the halves do not match. The rule is simple and unforgiving. Whatever follows the first half of the pair must be grammatically identical to whatever follows the second.",
   [["Which quotation explains how the sentence's structure supports its meaning?",['"It comes from Herodotus."','"The structure itself conveys relentlessness before the meaning arrives."','"A architect chose it in 1912."','"The rule is simple and unforgiving."'],1],
    ["What does the passage say about the phrase's official status?",["it is the USPS motto","it is not and never was an official motto","it was adopted in 1912","it is Persian law"],1],
    ["Why is 'not only reading but also to swim' incorrect?",["the meaning is unclear","the two halves are not grammatically identical","reading and swimming are unrelated","it is too short"],1],
    ["According to the passage, what do correlative conjunctions create?",["confusion","parallelism, and therefore emphasis","shorter sentences","questions"],1]]],

  20:["The Comma That Cost Ten Million Dollars","Informational",
   "In 2017, a United States federal appeals court decided a labor dispute on the basis of a missing comma.\n\nMaine law exempted from overtime pay workers involved in \"the canning, processing, preserving, freezing, drying, marketing, storing, packing for shipment or distribution of\" certain foods.\n\nThe dairy company argued the exemption covered distribution — that \"packing for shipment\" and \"distribution\" were two separate exempt activities. The drivers argued it covered only \"packing for shipment or distribution,\" a single activity, which they did not do.\n\nWith a comma after \"shipment,\" the company's reading would have been unambiguous. Without one, the phrase genuinely admits both readings.\n\nThe court sided with the drivers, and the company paid a settlement reported at five million dollars. Maine amended the statute the following year — not by adding the comma, but by rewriting the list with semicolons.\n\nThe case is now taught in law schools, but its lesson is broader than law. Punctuation in a series is not decoration. In a list of more than two items, the mark before the final conjunction resolves an ambiguity that the words themselves cannot. Most of the time that ambiguity is harmless and nobody notices.\n\nOccasionally it is worth several million dollars.",
   [["Which quotation identifies the specific ambiguity at issue?",['"In 2017, a United States federal appeals court decided a labor dispute."','"Without one, the phrase genuinely admits both readings."','"The case is now taught in law schools."','"Maine amended the statute the following year."'],1],
    ["How did Maine finally fix the statute?",["by adding the missing comma","by rewriting the list using semicolons","by repealing the law","by removing distribution"],1],
    ["What was the drivers' interpretation of the phrase?",["distribution was a separate exempt activity","'packing for shipment or distribution' was one single activity","the law did not apply to dairy","overtime was never required"],1],
    ["What broader lesson does the author draw?",["laws should be shorter","series punctuation resolves ambiguities the words alone cannot","courts make mistakes","commas are decorative"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 6 · Marks That Matter ------------------------------------- */
  21:["Before You Begin","Informational",
   "An introductory element is anything that comes before a sentence's main clause, and English asks you to mark where it ends.\n\nThe reason is practical. A reader moving left to right does not know, at the start of a sentence, whether the words they are encountering are the subject or a preliminary. Consider: \"While the family was eating the dog wandered in.\" For a fraction of a second, the family appears to be eating the dog. The comma after \"eating\" prevents that misreading entirely.\n\nLinguists call this a garden-path sentence — one that leads a reader down a wrong interpretation before forcing a backtrack. Punctuation exists in large part to prevent garden paths.\n\nThe convention covers more than clauses. Introductory prepositional phrases take a comma when they are long enough to cause a stumble. Introductory participial phrases always take one: \"Running down the hall, he tripped\" is correct, and removing the comma makes the hall appear to be running.\n\nTransition words — however, therefore, nevertheless, moreover — take a comma too, and for the same reason. They orient the reader to how this sentence relates to the last one before the sentence itself begins.\n\nThe underlying principle is not a rule to memorize. It is a courtesy: tell the reader where the preliminary ends before you ask them to parse the main idea.",
   [["Which quotation states the underlying principle behind introductory commas?",['"An introductory element is anything that comes before a sentence\'s main clause."','"Tell the reader where the preliminary ends before you ask them to parse the main idea."','"Linguists call this a garden-path sentence."','"Transition words take a comma too."'],1],
    ["What is a garden-path sentence?",["a sentence about gardens","one that leads a reader into a wrong interpretation before forcing a backtrack","a very long sentence","a sentence with no punctuation"],1],
    ["According to the passage, when do introductory prepositional phrases take a comma?",["always","when they are long enough to cause a stumble","never","only in fiction"],1],
    ["Why do transition words like 'however' take a comma?",["they are long","they orient the reader to the relationship before the sentence begins","it is arbitrary","they are conjunctions"],1]]],

  22:["Let's Eat, Grandma","Literature",
   "Nadia's grandmother had taught English for thirty years and could not stop.\n\nThe family had learned to live with it. At dinner, if someone said \"me and Jonah went,\" she would repeat the sentence back correctly, mildly, as though continuing the conversation, and everyone would carry on.\n\nThe trouble came the summer Nadia turned twelve and decided to fight back.\n\nShe began leaving notes. The first said: \"Lets eat Grandma.\" Her grandmother found it on the refrigerator, read it, and laughed out loud for the first time in months — because without the comma and the apostrophe, the note proposed cannibalism, and Nadia knew exactly what she was doing.\n\nThe second note said: \"I'm sorry I love you.\" Her grandmother stared at that one a long time. With a comma after \"sorry,\" it is an apology followed by affection. Without one, it is something sadder and stranger — an apology for the love itself.\n\nShe never asked which Nadia had meant. She told Nadia later that she had decided the ambiguity was the point, and that in thirty years of teaching she had never received a better argument for punctuation than a twelve-year-old making her cry with a missing comma.\n\nThey wrote notes to each other for four more years.",
   [["Which quotation shows the grandmother's judgment about the second note?",['"She never asked which Nadia had meant."','"She had decided the ambiguity was the point."','"Her grandmother stared at that one a long time."','"They wrote notes to each other for four more years."'],1],
    ["Why is 'Lets eat Grandma' funny?",["it is misspelled","without the comma, Grandma becomes the meal rather than the person addressed","it is too short","grandmothers do not cook"],1],
    ["How does a comma after 'sorry' change the second note's meaning?",["it makes it longer","it separates an apology from a declaration of love, rather than apologizing for the love","it makes it a question","it has no effect"],1],
    ["What does the ending suggest about the relationship?",["it deteriorated","the exchange of notes became a lasting bond between them","Nadia stopped writing","the grandmother stopped teaching"],1]]],

  23:["Titles, and Why They Are Marked","Informational",
   "The convention for marking titles looks arbitrary until you notice the principle behind it: containers get italics, contents get quotation marks.\n\nA book is a container; a chapter is its content. A magazine is a container; an article is its content. An album contains songs, a television series contains episodes, a website contains pages. In every case the larger, standalone work is italicized, and the smaller piece housed inside it is placed in quotation marks.\n\nThis is why the same title can be marked differently depending on what you mean. Written in italics, a title refers to the whole album. In quotation marks, it refers to the single track that shares its name. The punctuation is doing real work — it tells the reader which of two different things you are pointing at.\n\nWhen writing by hand, underlining substitutes for italics. This is not a lesser option; it is the older one. Italic type was unavailable to anyone writing with a pen, and the underline was the typesetter's instruction to set that text in italics. The mark survived into handwriting because it always meant the same thing.\n\nGet this wrong and the sentence still communicates. Get it right and the reader knows precisely what you consulted.",
   [["Which quotation states the organizing principle for marking titles?",['"The convention for marking titles looks arbitrary."','"Containers get italics, contents get quotation marks."','"When writing by hand, underlining substitutes for italics."','"Get this wrong and the sentence still communicates."'],1],
    ["How should a chapter title be marked?",["italics","quotation marks","underlining","capitals"],1],
    ["Why does underlining substitute for italics in handwriting?",["it is easier","the underline was originally the typesetter's instruction to set text in italics","italics are modern","it looks better"],1],
    ["What does the passage say the punctuation accomplishes?",["decoration","it tells the reader which of two different things you are pointing at","it shortens sentences","it marks importance"],1]]],

  24:["The Sentence He Rewrote Forty Times","Literature",
   "Mr. Adeyemi taught writing by making his students shorten things.\n\nThe assignment that made him locally famous was the forty-word paragraph. Students wrote a paragraph of any length about something that mattered to them. Then they cut it to eighty words without losing anything essential. Then to forty. Then — for anyone who wanted to — to twenty.\n\nMost students discovered around the eighty mark that a third of their original words had been doing nothing. \"Due to the fact that\" became \"because.\" \"In the event that\" became \"if.\" Sentences that had opened with throat-clearing began instead with their actual subject.\n\nThe hard part came later. Between forty and twenty, students had to start cutting things they liked. Adeyemi told them this was the point. Anyone can cut what is obviously useless. Judgment begins when the thing you are cutting is good, and you have to decide whether it earns its space.\n\nHis own example, which he showed every year, was a paragraph about his father that he had rewritten forty times over eleven years. The final version was two sentences.\n\nHe told students he was not sure it was better than the twelfth version. He said he was sure that writing the other twenty-eight had taught him what the two sentences meant.",
   [["Which quotation states where Adeyemi believes real judgment begins?",['"Anyone can cut what is obviously useless."','"Judgment begins when the thing you are cutting is good, and you have to decide whether it earns its space."','"Most students discovered around the eighty mark..."','"The final version was two sentences."'],1],
    ["What did students typically discover at the eighty-word mark?",["their paragraphs were too short","about a third of their original words were doing nothing","they disliked writing","forty words was impossible"],1],
    ["What does 'throat-clearing' refer to in this passage?",["illness","empty opening words before the real subject","speaking aloud","punctuation"],1],
    ["What does Adeyemi conclude about his forty revisions?",["the last version is clearly best","the discarded versions taught him what the final two sentences meant","revision is wasteful","eleven years was too long"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 7 · Roots of Meaning -------------------------------------- */
  25:["The Hundred Keys","Informational",
   "English contains well over a million words, and no one learns them one at a time.\n\nRoughly sixty percent of English vocabulary derives from Latin and Greek, and the proportion rises sharply in academic and scientific writing — closer to ninety percent in some technical fields. This means that a student who learns a few hundred roots and affixes acquires a tool that generates meaning for tens of thousands of words they have never encountered.\n\nConsider the root spect, meaning look. It yields inspect, spectator, spectacle, prospect, retrospect, perspective, conspicuous, circumspect, and speculate. A student who knows the root and a handful of prefixes can approach an unfamiliar member of that family and produce a reasonable guess: circum means around, so circumspect means looking around — cautious.\n\nThis is not merely a vocabulary trick. It changes what a reader can do alone. A student without morphological knowledge, meeting an unknown word, has two options: skip it or look it up. A student with it has a third: reason it out and keep reading.\n\nResearchers who study reading call this generative knowledge — knowledge that produces more knowledge without further instruction. Very little of what schools teach qualifies. Roots do.",
   [["Which quotation best supports the claim that root knowledge changes a reader's independence?",['"English contains well over a million words."','"A student with it has a third: reason it out and keep reading."','"Roughly sixty percent of English vocabulary derives from Latin and Greek."','"Roots do."'],1],
    ["What does 'generative knowledge' mean in this passage?",["knowledge about generators","knowledge that produces more knowledge without further instruction","knowledge for young people","general knowledge"],1],
    ["Using the passage's method, what would 'retrospect' most likely mean?",["looking around","looking back","looking forward","looking through"],1],
    ["Why does the author say the proportion of Latin and Greek words rises in academic writing?",["academics prefer old words","technical fields draw more heavily on classical roots","English is changing","translations are common"],1]]],

  26:["The Word That Meant the Opposite","Informational",
   "Some English words now mean the reverse of what they originally meant, and the drift is usually invisible while it happens.\n\n\"Nice\" descends from the Latin nescius, meaning ignorant. It passed through senses of foolish, then fussy, then precise — a survival visible in the phrase \"a nice distinction,\" which means a fine or exact one, not a pleasant one. Only recently did it settle into agreeable.\n\n\"Awful\" once meant full of awe, and was a compliment. \"Terrific\" meant causing terror. \"Egregious\" meant standing out from the flock — remarkably good — before sarcastic use flipped it to remarkably bad, and the sarcasm wore off leaving only the reversal.\n\nLinguists call this semantic drift, and it is not decay. It is what living languages do. A word's meaning is a record of how a community has actually used it, and communities change.\n\nThis matters for reading older texts. A reader who encounters \"awful majesty\" in an eighteenth-century sermon and imports the modern sense will misread the sentence completely — and will not know they have, because the sentence still parses.\n\nThat is the danger of drift. It does not produce nonsense that alerts you. It produces a plausible wrong meaning.",
   [["Which quotation identifies the specific danger of semantic drift for readers?",['"Linguists call this semantic drift, and it is not decay."','"It produces a plausible wrong meaning."','"\'Awful\' once meant full of awe."','"Communities change."'],1],
    ["What did 'egregious' originally mean?",["remarkably bad","standing out from the flock — remarkably good","ordinary","frightening"],1],
    ["What does the phrase 'a nice distinction' preserve?",["the modern meaning of pleasant","an older sense meaning fine or exact","a Latin spelling","a sarcastic use"],1],
    ["Why does the author say drift 'does not produce nonsense that alerts you'?",["the words are misspelled","the sentence still parses, so the reader does not notice the misreading","readers are careless","old texts are unclear"],1]]],

  27:["Building Words Like Machines","Informational",
   "The word antidisestablishmentarianism is usually offered as a curiosity, but it is better understood as a demonstration.\n\nStrip it apart. The root is establish. Add -ment and it becomes a noun: establishment, the institution. Add dis- and you get disestablishment, the act of removing that institution's official status. Add anti- and you get opposition to that removal. Add -arian and you have a person holding that position. Add -ism and you have the doctrine itself.\n\nEvery layer is a standard English operation, applied in sequence. Nothing about the word is irregular. It is long only because six operations were stacked.\n\nThis is what makes affixes powerful: they compose. A reader who knows each piece can disassemble a word they have never seen and reassemble its meaning, without a dictionary and without having been taught that particular word.\n\nThe skill transfers directly to academic reading, where long words are common and are almost always built rather than invented. Incomprehensibility, counterproductive, misrepresentation, unsustainable — each is a short familiar root wearing a stack of standard parts.\n\nStudents who freeze at long words are usually not defeated by length. They are defeated by treating the word as a single unknown thing rather than as an assembly.",
   [["Which quotation states the passage's main claim about affixes?",['"The word antidisestablishmentarianism is usually offered as a curiosity."','"This is what makes affixes powerful: they compose."','"Every layer is a standard English operation."','"Nothing about the word is irregular."'],1],
    ["According to the passage, why do students freeze at long words?",["the words are genuinely irregular","they treat the word as one unknown thing rather than an assembly","they read too slowly","the words are rare"],1],
    ["What does the suffix -arian add in this example?",["a doctrine","a person holding the position","an action","opposition"],1],
    ["What does the word 'compose' mean as used here?",["write music","combine in sequence to build meaning","calm down","arrange visually"],1]]],

  28:["The Bank of the River","Literature",
   "The English test had one question Amara could not stop thinking about afterward.\n\nIt gave a sentence — \"He sat on the bank and counted what he had left\" — and asked which meaning of bank applied.\n\nThe obvious answer was riverbank. The sentence had a man sitting, and people sit on riverbanks. But counted what he had left pulled the other direction, toward money, and Amara had sat with the question for four full minutes while the clock ran.\n\nWhat she eventually wrote in the margin, and what her teacher later read aloud to the class, was this: that the sentence did not contain enough information, and that a well-written passage would have supplied it, and that the ambiguity was probably deliberate on the test-writer's part but was a flaw in the sentence rather than a subtlety in it.\n\nShe chose riverbank and got the mark.\n\nHer teacher told her afterward that the margin note was worth more than the answer. Multiple-meaning words, she said, are only resolvable by context — and a writer who supplies a multiple-meaning word without enough context has not written a puzzle. They have written a mistake.\n\nAmara has never once written the word bank since without checking the sentence around it.",
   [["Which quotation states Amara's actual objection to the test question?",['"She chose riverbank and got the mark."','"The ambiguity was probably deliberate on the test-writer\'s part but was a flaw in the sentence rather than a subtlety in it."','"The obvious answer was riverbank."','"Amara had sat with the question for four full minutes."'],1],
    ["What pulled Amara toward the financial meaning of 'bank'?",["the word 'sat'","the phrase 'counted what he had left'","the word 'he'","the test instructions"],1],
    ["What does the teacher conclude about multiple-meaning words?",["they should never be used","they are only resolvable by context, so a writer must supply enough of it","they are always mistakes","students should memorize them"],1],
    ["What lasting effect did the question have on Amara?",["she stopped taking tests","she checks the surrounding context whenever she writes an ambiguous word","she avoids the word bank","she distrusts her teacher"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 8 · Figures and Nuance ------------------------------------ */
  29:["What the Metaphor Does","Informational",
   "A metaphor is often explained as a decoration — a fancier way of saying something plain. That explanation is wrong, and it makes metaphor harder to understand than it needs to be.\n\nA metaphor imports structure. When a writer says an argument was demolished, they are not merely saying it failed. They are importing an entire framework from the domain of buildings: arguments have foundations, they can be shaky, they can be propped up, they can collapse. The single word demolished carries all of that with it.\n\nResearchers who study language have documented how deeply this runs. Speakers of English routinely discuss time as though it were space — a long meeting, a short wait, the week ahead, looking back. Almost nobody notices, because the metaphor is not decorating the thought. It is doing the thinking.\n\nThis is why a well-chosen metaphor can explain a difficult idea faster than a paragraph of literal description. It hands the reader a structure they already possess and says: the new thing works like this.\n\nAnd it is why a badly chosen one causes lasting confusion. Import the wrong structure and the reader will reason correctly from a false map, which is worse than having no map at all.",
   [["Which quotation states what a metaphor actually does, according to the author?",['"A metaphor is often explained as a decoration."','"A metaphor imports structure."','"Almost nobody notices."','"Researchers who study language have documented how deeply this runs."'],1],
    ["Why is a badly chosen metaphor worse than none at all?",["it sounds unpleasant","the reader reasons correctly from a false map","it takes longer to read","it is confusing to write"],1],
    ["What example does the author give of a metaphor nobody notices?",["arguments as buildings","time discussed as though it were space","life as a journey","ideas as light"],1],
    ["What does 'imports' most nearly mean in this passage?",["brings in from another country","brings in a framework from another domain","emphasizes","exaggerates"],1]]],

  30:["The Proverb and the Exception","Literature",
   "Grandmother Yusuf had a proverb for everything, and her grandson Ibrahim had spent a year collecting the contradictions.\n\nHe presented them at dinner, on a list.\n\nLook before you leap — but he who hesitates is lost. Many hands make light work — but too many cooks spoil the broth. Absence makes the heart grow fonder — but out of sight, out of mind.\n\n\"So they cancel out,\" he said. \"They can't all be true.\"\n\nHis grandmother read the list carefully and set it down.\n\n\"You have proved that a proverb is not a law,\" she said. \"Good. Nobody said it was.\"\n\nShe explained it this way. A law claims to hold always. A proverb claims something narrower and more useful: that in a certain kind of situation, a certain danger is common and easy to miss. Look before you leap is for people who are about to rush. He who hesitates is lost is for people who are about to stall. They do not contradict each other because they are not addressed to the same person at the same moment.\n\n\"The skill,\" she said, \"is not memorizing them. It is knowing which one you need today. That is what wisdom is, and no list can do it for you.\"\n\nIbrahim kept the list. He added her explanation at the top.",
   [["Which quotation states the grandmother's central distinction?",['"You have proved that a proverb is not a law."','"A law claims to hold always. A proverb claims something narrower."','"So they cancel out."','"Ibrahim kept the list."'],1],
    ["According to the grandmother, why do contradictory proverbs not actually conflict?",["one of each pair is false","they address different people in different situations","they are from different countries","proverbs are meaningless"],1],
    ["What does the grandmother identify as the real skill?",["memorizing proverbs","knowing which proverb a given moment requires","collecting contradictions","writing them down"],1],
    ["What theme does this passage develop?",["old sayings are unreliable","wisdom is knowing how to apply a principle, not merely knowing principles","grandparents are always right","lists are useful"],1]]],

  31:["The Difference Between Thrifty and Cheap","Informational",
   "Two words can point at the identical behavior and pass opposite verdicts on it.\n\nThrifty and stingy both describe a person reluctant to spend. So do frugal, economical, tight-fisted, prudent, and miserly. A person who declines an expensive dinner has performed one action; which of those seven words a witness reaches for reveals what the witness thinks of them.\n\nLinguists separate two layers here. Denotation is what a word points at — its dictionary definition. Connotation is the attitude it carries. Thrifty and stingy share a denotation almost exactly. Their connotations are opposed.\n\nThis is why the choice of a synonym is never neutral, and why writers who claim to be reporting are sometimes arguing. A crowd can be described as a gathering, an assembly, a mob, or a throng. All four denote many people in one place. Only one convicts them.\n\nCareful readers learn to notice this. When a text repeatedly selects the harsher member of each synonym pair, something is being done to you, and it is being done below the level of the claims you could argue with.\n\nThe defense is simple: ask what the plainest available word would have been, and why it was not used.",
   [["Which quotation states the passage's warning to readers?",['"Two words can point at the identical behavior."','"When a text repeatedly selects the harsher member of each synonym pair, something is being done to you."','"Denotation is what a word points at."','"The defense is simple."'],1],
    ["What is the difference between denotation and connotation?",["there is none","denotation is what a word points at; connotation is the attitude it carries","denotation is modern, connotation old","connotation is the dictionary meaning"],1],
    ["Of gathering, assembly, mob and throng, which does the author say 'convicts them'?",["gathering","assembly","mob","throng"],2],
    ["What defense does the author recommend?",["avoid synonyms","ask what the plainest available word would have been, and why it was not used","read only facts","use a dictionary"],1]]],

  32:["The Two Voices of Marcus Bell","Literature",
   "Marcus spoke one way at his grandmother's table and another way in the courtroom, and for most of law school he was ashamed of it.\n\nHe had grown up in a household with its own grammar — a dialect with rules as consistent as any other, though the rules were not the ones tested on standardized examinations. At eighteen he learned a second set. By twenty-five he could move between them without thinking, and had come to regard the first as something to be hidden.\n\nWhat changed his mind was a linguistics course he took almost by accident.\n\nThe professor's argument was simple. Every dialect has systematic rules. None is logically superior. What varies is not correctness but prestige — which variety a society has decided to associate with education and power. That is a fact about the society, not about the dialect.\n\nMarcus found this genuinely difficult. He had built a career on the second variety and had been told his whole life that it was the right one.\n\nHe came to a position he still holds. Formal English is worth mastering because it opens doors, and pretending otherwise does young people no favors. But mastering it is adding a tool, not correcting a defect — and the voice at his grandmother's table was never broken.\n\nHe uses both. He is ashamed of neither.",
   [["Which quotation states the professor's central argument?",['"Every dialect has systematic rules. None is logically superior."','"Marcus spoke one way at his grandmother\'s table and another way in the courtroom."','"He uses both."','"Marcus found this genuinely difficult."'],0],
    ["What distinction does Marcus finally reach?",["formal English is superior","mastering formal English adds a tool rather than correcting a defect","dialects should replace formal English","he should choose one voice"],1],
    ["According to the passage, what does prestige reflect?",["a dialect's logical structure","a society's decisions about which variety to associate with education and power","grammatical correctness","vocabulary size"],1],
    ["Why does the author say pretending formal English does not matter 'does young people no favors'?",["it is grammatically wrong","it opens doors, so withholding it has real costs","dialects are inferior","teachers require it"],1]]],

  /* ---- Unit 9 · Make the Case ---------------------------------------- */
  33:["Three Sources, One Question","Informational",
   "A student researching whether school start times should be moved later will find, within ten minutes, three sources that appear to conflict.\n\nThe first is a sleep researcher's study finding that adolescent circadian rhythms shift roughly two hours later at puberty, and that later start times correlate with improved attendance and test scores.\n\nThe second is a school district's report concluding that a proposed change would cost 1.8 million dollars annually in additional transportation, because buses currently run three staggered routes.\n\nThe third is a parent survey in which 61 percent opposed the change, citing after-school employment and childcare.\n\nA weak researcher picks the source that agrees with them and cites it. A slightly better one cites all three and concludes that the question is complicated.\n\nThe strongest move is to notice that the three sources are not actually answering the same question. The first addresses whether later start times benefit students biologically. The second addresses what one specific implementation would cost. The third addresses whether one community currently wants it.\n\nAll three can be entirely true simultaneously. A researcher who sees this can write something genuinely useful: that the biological case is strong, the logistical obstacles are real and specific, and the opposition is grounded in consequences the biological research does not address.\n\nThat is not fence-sitting. It is accuracy.",
   [["Which quotation identifies the strongest research move?",['"A weak researcher picks the source that agrees with them."','"The three sources are not actually answering the same question."','"All three can be entirely true simultaneously."','"That is not fence-sitting."'],1],
    ["What question does the district's report actually address?",["whether later starts help students biologically","what one specific implementation would cost","whether parents want the change","how buses are designed"],1],
    ["Why does the author say concluding 'the question is complicated' is only 'slightly better'?",["it is false","it stops short of identifying why the sources differ","it is too short","it ignores the parents"],1],
    ["What distinction does the final line draw?",["between research and opinion","between fence-sitting and accuracy","between students and parents","between cost and benefit"],1]]],

  34:["The Footnote That Saved a Reputation","Informational",
   "In 2011, a graduate student checking citations in a widely cited psychology paper found that one of its central claims traced back to nothing.\n\nThe paper asserted a specific statistic and cited an earlier study. That earlier study cited a still earlier one. The third cited a conference presentation. The presentation, when finally located, described the figure as a rough estimate offered for discussion, based on no data collection at all.\n\nThe practice has a name: citation laundering. A tentative claim is cited, then the citation is cited, and with each step the hedging drops away. By the fourth generation, an offhand guess has become an established fact with an impressive trail of references behind it.\n\nNothing in the chain was fraudulent. Each author cited a real source that really said what they claimed. The failure was that nobody followed the chain to its origin.\n\nThis is the practical reason scholarship demands source lists rather than assertions. A citation is not a decoration or a defense against accusations of plagiarism. It is a road, and its purpose is that someone can walk back down it.\n\nA claim you cannot trace is not knowledge. It is a rumor with formatting.",
   [["Which quotation states the true purpose of a citation?",['"A citation is not a decoration."','"It is a road, and its purpose is that someone can walk back down it."','"The practice has a name: citation laundering."','"Nothing in the chain was fraudulent."'],1],
    ["What is citation laundering?",["deliberate fraud","a tentative claim losing its hedging as it is cited repeatedly","copying without credit","citing too many sources"],1],
    ["Why does the author emphasize that 'nothing in the chain was fraudulent'?",["to excuse the authors","to show the failure was procedural rather than dishonest","to accuse the graduate student","to defend the journal"],1],
    ["What does the final line mean?",["rumors should be formatted","an untraceable claim only resembles knowledge","formatting matters most","knowledge is rare"],1]]],

  35:["The Strongest Version","Literature",
   "Debate practice at Millbrook had one rule that new students always hated.\n\nBefore you argued your position, you had to state your opponent's position — and they had to agree that you had stated it fairly. If they did not agree, you tried again. You did not get to argue until they were satisfied.\n\nFor the first month, Teodora found it maddening. She had come to win. Restating her opponent's argument in its strongest form felt like handing them ammunition.\n\nThe change came slowly. She noticed that when she was forced to build the other side properly, she often found that the version she had planned to attack was not the version anyone actually held. Her prepared rebuttals were aimed at a target she had constructed.\n\nHer coach called this steelmanning, the opposite of the more familiar straw man. A straw man is an argument built to be knocked down. A steel man is your opponent's case built as well as they could build it — or better.\n\n\"It costs you nothing you should want to keep,\" the coach said. \"If your position only wins against the weak version, you did not have a position. You had a preference.\"\n\nTeodora lost more rounds that year than the year before. She has said since that it was the year she learned to think.",
   [["Which quotation states the coach's central argument for the rule?",['"You did not get to argue until they were satisfied."','"If your position only wins against the weak version, you did not have a position. You had a preference."','"A straw man is an argument built to be knocked down."','"Teodora lost more rounds that year."'],1],
    ["What did Teodora discover when forced to build the opposing case properly?",["her opponents were dishonest","the version she planned to attack was often not the one anyone held","debate was pointless","she always won"],1],
    ["What is the difference between a straw man and a steel man?",["there is none","a straw man is built to be knocked down; a steel man is the opponent's case at its strongest","one is written, one spoken","a steel man is dishonest"],1],
    ["What does the final line suggest about the value of losing more rounds?",["she should have quit","genuine thinking mattered more to her than the win record","the rule was unfair","she was poorly coached"],1]]],

  36:["Say It So They Can Follow","Informational",
   "The single most common failure in student presentations is not nervousness. It is that the speaker knows the material too well.\n\nWhen you have spent three weeks with a topic, its structure feels obvious. You forget that your audience is meeting it for the first time and in a fixed order they cannot control. A reader who loses the thread can go back a paragraph. A listener cannot. Everything a listener understands, they must understand in the sequence you deliver it, at the speed you deliver it.\n\nThis has practical consequences. Signpost more than feels necessary — state your three points before making them, and name each one as you arrive. Repetition that would be tiresome on the page is often exactly right aloud.\n\nSlow down more than feels natural. Nervous speakers accelerate, and the material they know best is where they accelerate most, because it requires no thought from them. It requires plenty from the audience.\n\nUse a visual display to carry what the ear handles badly: numbers, sequences, relationships between parts. Do not use one to duplicate your sentences, because an audience reading your slides is not listening to you.\n\nAnd when someone asks a question you cannot answer, say so. An honest \"I don't know, but here is how I would find out\" builds more credibility than any confident guess, because it tells the audience your other answers were real.",
   [["Which quotation identifies the passage's central diagnosis?",['"The single most common failure in student presentations is not nervousness."','"It is that the speaker knows the material too well."','"Slow down more than feels natural."','"Use a visual display."'],1],
    ["Why does the author say listeners are more constrained than readers?",["they are less intelligent","they must understand in the sequence and at the speed delivered","they cannot see the speaker","they are distracted"],1],
    ["According to the passage, where do nervous speakers accelerate most?",["at the beginning","on material they know best","during questions","at the conclusion"],1],
    ["Why does admitting you do not know an answer build credibility?",["it shows humility","it tells the audience your other answers were real","audiences expect it","it saves time"],1]]]
  };

  function passageFor(week){
    const r=P[week];
    if(!r) return null;
    const spine=(window.__CURR.LA_Y2 && window.__CURR.LA_Y2.WEEKS)||[];
    const wk=spine.find(w=>w.n===week);
    return {
      id:"la-y2-w"+week+"-reading", w:week, title:r[0], type:r[1],
      label:r[1]+': "'+r[0]+'"',
      text:r[2],
      readAloud:"Read this aloud once before answering. At this level, watch your pace and expression — you are reading for meaning, not speed.",
      unit: wk?wk.unit:null,
      questions: r[3].map((q,i)=>({
        id:"la-y2-rd-w"+week+"-q"+(i+1),
        type:"multiple-choice",
        t: i===0?1 : i===r[3].length-1?2 : 1,
        q:q[0], options:q[1], a:q[2]
      }))
    };
  }

  window.__CURR = window.__CURR || {};
  window.__CURR.LA_Y2 = Object.assign(window.__CURR.LA_Y2||{}, {PASSAGE_ROWS:P, passageFor});
})();
